    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2026 EICMA's Speed Legends Exhibit Was Not To Be Missed – See the Bikes That Paved MotoGP History\nHonda Is Recalling the S2000 Roadster for Fuel Flange Joint Leak Risk\n7 photos\nPhoto: Honda / edited\nHonda has identified a rather worrying issue affecting one of the company's most celebrated production models. According to American Honda Motor, an estimated 16,387 examples of the S2K may feature replacement fuel flange joints without the brazing material designed to secure the joint-to-flange connection.\nAHM claims the subject parts could loosen due to vibration, separate completely, allowing dinosaur juice to escape. This could either starve the engine of gasoline, bringing the two-seat roadster to an unexpected stop. Warnings include fuel odor and leakage, reduced acceleration response, misfiring, stalling, and the failure to restart. Sanoh Industrial is the supplier of the subject component.\nPart number 16623-PCX-003 is also listed in the recall documentation, where American Honda Motor explains that a customer in Japan prompted the Japanese automaker to investigate. Said customer experienced symptoms in November 2025. Two months later, a used vehicle was sourced for testing.\nThe Japanese automaker conducted tests and analyzed the concern through May 2026. A brazing identification method for potentially affected components was developed in July 2026. Between August and October, the company investigated the potentially affected S2000 population, including cars potentially repaired with the defective assembly.\nThankfully for everyone, American Honda Motor has not received any warranty claims or field reports potentially related to the described concern. Referred to as 26V749 by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, this recall involves 2004 and 2005 models.\nPhoto: Honda That means all suspect vehicles are AP2s, namely the S2000 with the larger-displacement engine that is better in terms of low- and mid-range torque. The 2.2-liter mill doesn't rev as high as the 2.0-liter screamer of the AP1, but nevertheless, the AP2 prides itself on shorter lower gears, a taller sixth, carbon-fiber synchros, revised suspension, and larger wheels.\nHonda further worked its magic in terms of styling, updating both the front and rear bumpers, the headlights, taillights, and exhaust finishers. Objectively, the AP2 is the better all-around package while the AP1 is the more intense, purer sports car. Also worth noting, the subject 2004 and 2005 models are AP2s with a traditional cable throttle and no traction control.\nStarting with model year 2026, the US-market S2K switched to an electronic throttle control system that still uses a cable to connect the loud pedal to the sensor. Vehicle Stability Assist, meanwhile, combines stability and traction control. As you'd expect from such a magnificent sports car, VSA can be turned off.\nHonda ultimately identified 67 replacement parts that its supplier manufactured without the required brazing operation, specifically the process where two or more metal components are joined by melting a filler metal into the joint. Said material reinforces the joint-to-flange connection, but due to the potential lack of material, the press-fit connection is vulnerable to loosening and separation.\nSubject vehicles were produced between April 14, 2003, and September 21, 2005. The S2000 was produced at the Takanezawa and Suzuka plants through August 2009, with Suzuka taking over from Takanezawa in May 2004. Just under 110,700 units were produced in total, and over half were sent to the US market. Per the Part 573 safety recall report, owners will be notified in January 2027.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHonda S2000 Honda recall US safety\nDownload: Honda S2000 Fuel Flange Joint Leak Risk Recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT For True Outdoor Lovers: inTech's Tiny 2027 Flyer Explore Toy Hauler Doesn't Sacrifice Garage Space",
